		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Etiquette Settings]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="http://localhost/opengd77/viewtopic.php?t=5084&amp;p=36915#p36915"><![CDATA[
Such requests are probably because most networks still recommend setting radios admit criteria to be 'polite to colour code' . This means a radio is not allowed to transmit over another radio on the same colour code.  <br>The main reason for recommending this is historical.  The commercial radios (used when the DMR networks were originally set up) only receive the talkgroup  they are currently sending (or maybe a small list of talkgroups). If a different talkgroup is in use then the radio will be silent and an untrained user may not realise the channel is in use and try to transmit.<br><br>In theory 'polite to colour code' sounds simple, just prevent transmission if you are receiving a signal on your own colour code. However it is actually more complicated than that as it has to cope with repeater use which needs to allow a radio to sometimes  break this rule. A radio <strong class="text-strong"><span style="text-decoration:underline">is</span></strong>allowed to transmit during the repeaters 'hang time' if it is using the same talkgroup as the previous transmission. It is also allowed to transmit if the opposite timeslot is busy but the selected timeslot is not. Both of these conditions need the radio to analyse the channel control data from the repeater before allowing transmission. This is where many Chinese radios get it wrong. <br><br>OpenGD77 operates by default with the DMR filter off, so a user will hear any transmission on any talkgroup and should realise the channel is in use. This is the recommended way of operating and makes the admit criteria less necessary when using this firmware.<br><br>Colin G4EML<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=87">G4EML</a> — Sat Apr 26, 2025 11:20 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
